1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:04,415
Think of a leader who you admire and
respect.

2
00:00:04,415 --> 00:00:07,090
What qualities do they have
that makes them a good leader?

3
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:10,219
Maybe they're an active listener.

4
00:00:10,219 --> 00:00:13,200
Maybe they're great at fostering a sense
of community for people around them.

5
00:00:14,450 --> 00:00:18,275
As a project manager,
leadership is a big part of the job.

6
00:00:18,275 --> 00:00:21,977
But being a great project manager is
about more than simply leading a team to

7
00:00:21,977 --> 00:00:23,920
complete a project.

8
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:27,981
It's about supporting the people on
your team to do their best work, and

9
00:00:27,981 --> 00:00:30,922
enabling people to build
things they're proud of.

10
00:00:30,922 --> 00:00:34,618
In this video, we'll define teamwork and
explain why it's important for

11
00:00:34,618 --> 00:00:36,329
successful project management.

12
00:00:37,590 --> 00:00:41,141
The best leaders are able to
get people to work together.

13
00:00:41,141 --> 00:00:43,993
That's why a project manager's
ability to develop and

14
00:00:43,993 --> 00:00:47,510
lead teams that work well together is so
important.

15
00:00:47,510 --> 00:00:53,933
But before we go further, let's pause and
consider, what exactly is a team?

16
00:00:53,933 --> 00:00:56,910
Though there are many different
definitions of the word team,

17
00:00:56,910 --> 00:00:58,940
here's how we describe it here at Google.

18
00:01:00,030 --> 00:01:05,862
A team is a group of people who plan,
solve problems, make decisions, and

19
00:01:05,862 --> 00:01:11,059
review progress in service of
a specific project, or objective.

20
00:01:11,059 --> 00:01:14,290
Team members rely on each
other to get things done.

21
00:01:14,290 --> 00:01:19,184
For example, a software engineering
team might be tasked with creating one

22
00:01:19,184 --> 00:01:22,787
seamless software experience
that meets users needs.

23
00:01:22,787 --> 00:01:26,239
Beyond coordinating with the project
manager members of the software

24
00:01:26,239 --> 00:01:30,217
engineering team will also coordinate with
one another as they work toward their

25
00:01:30,217 --> 00:01:31,550
shared goal.

26
00:01:31,550 --> 00:01:33,744
Teams differ from workgroups,

27
00:01:33,744 --> 00:01:38,956
which we define as being based on
organizational or managerial hierarchy.

28
00:01:38,956 --> 00:01:42,688
Though people within a workgroup might
be working toward a common goal,

29
00:01:42,688 --> 00:01:45,747
their work is more likely to
be coordinated, controlled or

30
00:01:45,747 --> 00:01:48,580
assigned by single person or entity.

31
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:53,191
For example, a workgroup focused on
quality assurance testing might be

32
00:01:53,191 --> 00:01:55,770
tasked with running a set of test plans.

33
00:01:55,770 --> 00:01:58,776
The group manager might
split up that work and

34
00:01:58,776 --> 00:02:02,506
assign each quality assurance
tester a plan to review.

35
00:02:02,506 --> 00:02:03,598
But unlike a team,

36
00:02:03,598 --> 00:02:08,460
the testers will work independently of
one another to complete their tasks.

37
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:11,388
And will coordinate mainly
with the manager and

38
00:02:11,388 --> 00:02:14,673
the development team of
the feature they're testing.

39
00:02:14,673 --> 00:02:19,780
Teams and work groups each have unique
benefits within a larger organization.

40
00:02:19,780 --> 00:02:23,296
But for the purpose of this program,
we'll focus on teams.

41
00:02:23,296 --> 00:02:26,961
More specifically, we'll discuss
how project managers develop and

42
00:02:26,961 --> 00:02:30,010
lead effective teams by
fostering a culture of teamwork.

43
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:35,501
Teamwork is an effective
collaborative way of working in which

44
00:02:35,501 --> 00:02:39,652
each person is committed to and
heading toward a shared goal.

45
00:02:39,652 --> 00:02:44,285
Teamwork maximizes the individual
strength of each team member to

46
00:02:44,285 --> 00:02:46,690
bring out the best in each person.

47
00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:47,792
As you might imagine,

48
00:02:47,792 --> 00:02:51,750
teamwork is a crucial part of
successful project management.

49
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:53,876
There are a few reasons for this.

50
00:02:53,876 --> 00:02:57,080
For one, teamwork fosters creativity.

51
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:00,232
A team might leverage the diverse
perspectives, skills and

52
00:03:00,232 --> 00:03:03,385
experiences of their members
to devise better solutions and

53
00:03:03,385 --> 00:03:07,525
build products that address more diverse
user needs than they might have if they

54
00:03:07,525 --> 00:03:09,842
were working independently of each other.

55
00:03:09,842 --> 00:03:12,950
Teamwork also encourages accountability.

56
00:03:12,950 --> 00:03:17,878
The knowledge that the execution of
your tasks directly impacts the tasks of

57
00:03:17,878 --> 00:03:21,040
the rest of the team can
be a powerful motivator.

58
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:25,580
And most importantly,
teamwork helps you get stuff done.

59
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:30,510
Big complex projects need smart,
capable people to complete tasks and

60
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:33,807
to reach milestones to
meet the project goals.

61
00:03:33,807 --> 00:03:38,280
As the project manager, it's your job to
encourage others to actively engage in

62
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:42,756
working together as a team to foster
creativity, encourage accountability and

63
00:03:42,756 --> 00:03:43,830
to get stuff done.

64
00:03:45,030 --> 00:03:49,639
Teamwork is an effective collaborative
way of working and when done right,

65
00:03:49,639 --> 00:03:54,330
it can positively impact both measurable
team results and the team culture.

66
00:03:54,330 --> 00:03:58,003
Coming up, we'll take you through
the elements of effective teams.

67
00:03:58,003 --> 00:03:58,600
Meet you there.
